  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Do Neck Acupressure Points Matter?</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The neck is one of the most tension-prone regions of the body. Desk work, phone use, and everyday stress gradually pull the head forward and leave the muscles along the back and sides of the neck tight by the end of the day. That tightness typically shows up as aching, stiffness when turning the head, restricted movement, and headaches that start at the base of the skull before spreading upward. A small handful of points can speak to that entire pattern. The neck is also central to our guide to acupressure for neck pain, which combines these same locations with shoulder and scalp points for fuller coverage, and it overlaps with the headache and migraine guides.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">In TCM, the neck carries the Gallbladder and Bladder meridians along its sides, and keeping qì moving freely through the neck is seen as key to clearing tension that rises toward the head. Physically, the neck's large strap muscles and the attachment points at the skull base respond well to firm, sustained pressure, which can help tight muscle bands relax and supports better blood flow through the area.</p>
  </section>

