  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How Do You Stimulate Neck Points?</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Sit upright and use the pads of two or three fingers, pressing at a 90-degree angle and increasing the pressure slowly over 10–15 seconds. Hold for 30–60 seconds, or up to a full minute for deeper muscles, then release gradually while breathing evenly. On the front of the neck, avoid the pulse of the carotid artery and never grip or pinch near the throat — press muscle, never vessels, and keep the pressure gentle where the skin is thin.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can acupressure release chronic neck tension?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Acupressure is a reasonable tool for managing chronic neck tension, and many people feel looser after even one short session. Results tend to be cumulative: pressing the base-of-skull and shoulder-edge points for a minute or two daily, on both sides, is more realistic than expecting a single session to resolve a long-standing pattern. Combine it with posture breaks and gentle neck movement. If pain persists, worsens, or spreads into the arms, seek professional evaluation.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Where exactly is GB20?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">GB20 (Fengchi) lies in the two shallow depressions at the base of the skull, just off the midline, where the head meets the neck muscles. Slide your fingers up the back of the neck until they touch the skull, then walk them outward until they settle into the natural hollows on either side of the spine. Press there and you should feel a deep, dull ache rather than a sharp pinch. That soft depression is the point.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Hold each neck point for 30–60 seconds, pressing both sides where the point appears on both sides of the neck. Increase pressure gradually over 10–15 seconds until you feel a deep, dull sensation (deqi), and keep your breathing even throughout. You can repeat the sequence once or twice more. Stop if you feel sharp pain, tingling, or dizziness, and avoid pressing directly on the carotid pulse at the throat.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

